    "image_with_text_dbCme3": {
      "type": "image-with-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_AHXpTB": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Cut",
            "heading_size": "h1"
          }
        },
        "text_YJqfrB": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<ul><li>Out of all the 4Cs, the diamond’s “Cut” has the biggest impact on a diamond's beauty and sparkle. A well-cut diamond is proportional, symmetrical, and finished typically being the most expensive ones.</li><li>The precision and workmanship of a diamond's cut may either enhance or reduce its brightness and sparkle, hence increasing or decreasing the diamond's value.</li><li>The grades for diamond cuts under the GIA standard are Excellent, Very Good, Good, Fair, and Poor. Even a brilliant clarity diamond (no deformities or inclusions) might appear glassy or dull if the cut is excessively shallow or deep. So, when selecting a diamond, select one with the highest cut grade.</li></ul>",
            "text_style": "body"
          }
        },
        "caption_Q99tPg": {
          "type": "caption",
          "settings": {
            "caption": "Buying Tips:The most desirable for diamond to choose is round brilliant with 57 facets. For exceptional brilliance, keep your cut grade in the Excellent to Very Good range for a well-cut stone with accurate symmetry and polish.",
            "text_style": "subtitle",
            "text_size": "medium"
          }
        }
      },
